# the aarch64 assembly syntax, the vendor dialect
#
# every row's template and the index by mnemonic are generated from the
# instruction model's assembly symbols (gen_syntax): the template is the
# model's text in lower case, each symbol the overlay states as an operand a
# hole, and an optional `#` before an immediate a group with no operand. a za
# tile slice spells its tile by its number beside the slice's direction, as
# `za1v.s`, through the `number` format of a register hole.
#
# registers are written by their own names, x29 rather than fp, and read by
# those and their aliases, and mnemonics and names read without regard to case,
# as llvm-mc 23.1.1 reads and writes them.
#
# a register list hole's format is the element every member carries, as `.16b`,
# empty where its members carry none, as luti4's table pair `{ z16, z17 }`.
# its members read comma-separated or as a range `z0.b - z3.b` of consecutive
# numbers, which may wrap past the class's last register. they are written
# comma-separated, and as a range when the class is scalable and three or more
# members run up without wrapping, as llvm-mc writes `{ z0.b - z3.b }` and
# `{ v0.16b, v1.16b, v2.16b }`.
#
# a float immediate reads as a decimal number, as `#-1.25` or `#2`, and writes
# with eight places, more when its value needs them, as `#-1.25000000`.
#
# a system register reads by its name, or by its encoding as
# `s3_0_c15_c2_0`, op0, op1, CRn, CRm and op2, which Arm's register model
# gives as the spelling of the implementation defined space and llvm-mc reads
# for any encoding. one no row names writes that way.
#
# an operand the page spells by name or by number, as `dsb ish` or `dsb #12`,
# reads either way and writes by name where one names its value, by `#` and
# its decimal number otherwise, as llvm-mc 23.1.1 writes `dsb #12`.

